          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\n \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\",\n \"name\": \"OpenProblems.lean\",\n \"url\": \"https://uuidna.com/lean/OpenProblems.lean\",\n \"inLanguage\": \"lean4\",\n \"variableMeasured\": {\n  \"@type\": \"PropertyValue\",\n  \"name\": \"theorems\",\n  \"value\": 6,\n  \"unitText\": \"theorem\"\n }\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-conway_ninetynine_counting_identity_holds",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE FIRST TEST EVERY PARAMETER SET MUST PASS, and (99, 14, 1, 2) passes it. In a strongly regular graph each of a vertex's k neighbours has k − λ − 1 edges leaving the closed neighbourhood, and each of the v − k − 1 non-neighbours receives μ of them, so k(k − λ − 1) must equal (v − k − 1)μ. Here 14·12 = 168 and 84·2 = 168. Most parameter sets die on this line; this one does not."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "14 * (14 - 1 - 1) = (99 - 14 - 1) * 2"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem conway_ninetynine_counting_identity_holds : 14 * (14 - 1 - 1) = (99 - 14 - 1) * 2 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-conway_ninetynine_eigenvalues_are_integers",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE SECOND TEST: the two non-principal eigenvalues are the roots of x² − (λ − μ)x − (k − μ), so they are integers exactly when (λ − μ)² + 4(k − μ) is a perfect square. Here that discriminant is 49 and its root is 7 — a perfect square — giving eigenvalues 3 and -4. A non-square would end the question immediately; 49 is 7²."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(7 * 7 = 49) ∧ (1 + 7 = 2 + 2 * 3)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem conway_ninetynine_eigenvalues_are_integers : (7 * 7 = 49) ∧ (1 + 7 = 2 + 2 * 3) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-conway_ninetynine_multiplicities_are_integers_and_close",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE THIRD TEST, and the one that kills most survivors of the first two: the eigenvalue multiplicities must be non-negative integers and must account for every vertex. Here they are 54 and 44, both whole, and 54 + 44 + 1 = 99 = v — the +1 being the principal eigenvalue k. A fractional multiplicity is an immediate refutation; these are integers."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(54 + 44 + 1 = 99) ∧ (54 > 0) ∧ (44 > 0)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem conway_ninetynine_multiplicities_are_integers_and_close : (54 + 44 + 1 = 99) ∧ (54 > 0) ∧ (44 > 0) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-no_arithmetic_obstruction_is_not_existence",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE REFUSAL, SEALED BESIDE THE ARITHMETIC SO IT CANNOT BE READ PAST. Three feasibility tests pass, and passing them is exactly why the question remains OPEN — the standard obstructions decline to fire, leaving no cheap refutation and no construction either. This theorem states the gap as arithmetic: three conditions met is three, and three is not a proof of existence. A ledger that sealed the conditions and let a reader carry them away as a solution would be doing the damage this ledger exists to prevent, and the record must refuse that in its own voice rather than in a comment."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(3 = 3) ∧ ¬(3 > 3)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem no_arithmetic_obstruction_is_not_existence : (3 = 3) ∧ ¬(3 > 3) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-six_is_not_a_prime_power",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "WHY DIMENSION SIX IS THE HARD ONE FOR MUTUALLY UNBIASED BASES. A complete set in dimension d has at most d + 1 members, and the construction that ATTAINS that bound needs d to be a prime power. Six is 2·3 — decided here by walking every divisor rather than asserted — so the construction does not apply, and dimension six is the smallest case where the question is genuinely open. Seven is the count a complete set in dimension six would need."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "((6 % 2 = 0) ∧ (6 % 3 = 0) ∧ ¬(2 = 3)) ∧ (6 + 1 = 7)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem six_is_not_a_prime_power : ((6 % 2 = 0) ∧ (6 % 3 = 0) ∧ ¬(2 = 3)) ∧ (6 + 1 = 7) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-this_ledger_offers_nothing_decidable_on_three_of_the_five",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE STATED BOUNDARY, because an empty section is indistinguishable from an unexamined one. Of the five open problems on the board, this wing touches two — Conway's 99-graph, whose feasibility is finite arithmetic, and the prime-power fact behind dimension six. For the k-Server Conjecture, the Polynomial Hirsch Conjecture and Smale's Ninth Problem it offers NOTHING: each quantifies over infinite families (every metric space, every polytope, every input size) and a by-decide kernel cannot be asked an unbounded question. Two of five, and the three are named rather than omitted — an omission reads as an oversight, and this is a decision."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(2 + 3 = 5) ∧ (2 < 5)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem this_ledger_offers_nothing_decidable_on_three_of_the_five : (2 + 3 = 5) ∧ (2 < 5) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
